<div class="not-prose">
<p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">A car that hasn't moved in days, collecting leaves or snow, with expired tabs? Who you call depends on <strong class="text-white">where it's parked</strong>: on a public street, the city handles it. On your own property, you can have it towed yourself.</p><div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 border-l-4 border-l-red-500/60 rounded-lg p-5 my-6 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><em>Note: Spokane Towing is a towing referral line. We aren't the city, the police or an impound lot. This is a plain-language guide based on City of Spokane, City of Spokane Valley and Washington State sources, linked below. Rules can change, so check the source if anything is unclear.</em></div><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Quick answer: who to contact</h2><div class="overflow-x-auto my-6"><table class="w-full text-left text-sm border border-slate-800 rounded-lg"><thead class="bg-slate-900/70 text-white"><tr><th class="p-3">Where the vehicle is</th><th class="p-3">Who to contact</th></tr></thead><tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800 text-gray-300"><tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">A City of Spokane street</td><td class="p-3">File a Parking Violation Complaint at <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://myspokane311.my.site.com/citizen/s/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Spokane311</a>, or call 311 / (509) 755-2489, Mon–Fri, 7 a.m.–6 p.m. (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/news/stories/2026/11/10/think-the-vehicle-in-front-of-your-house-is-abandoned/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane, Nov. 2026</a>)</td></tr><tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">A Spokane Valley street or city property</td><td class="p-3">Spokane Valley Code Enforcement through <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://spokanevalleywa.gov/443/SVexpress---Report-a-Concern" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">SVexpress</a>, or call (509) 720-5000</td></tr><tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">Private property in Spokane Valley</td><td class="p-3">Report it to Crime Check, (509) 456-2233, so police can check whether it's stolen. The property owner is generally responsible for removal (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://www.spokanevalleywa.gov/315/Addressing-Homelessness-for-Businesses-a" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane Valley</a>)</td></tr><tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">Your own driveway, yard or lot</td><td class="p-3">You can have a registered tow operator remove it. See “Abandoned car on your property” below.</td></tr><tr><td class="p-3 align-top font-semibold text-white">Blocking traffic, or an emergency</td><td class="p-3">Call 911</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">When is a car on a Spokane street “abandoned”?</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">Inside the City of Spokane, parking time limits are set by <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/smc/?Find=park&Section=16A.05.310" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">SMC 16A.05.310</a>:</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6"><div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Passenger cars, trucks, SUVs: 72 hours</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">No more than 72 continuous hours on the same block face outside the downtown Paid Parking Zone, unless a sign gives a shorter limit.</p></div><div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">RVs, boats and trailers: 24 hours</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">Non-passenger vehicles are limited to 24 continuous hours on a block face.</p></div><div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Downtown Paid Parking Zone: 24 hours</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">No vehicle can sit on one block face for more than 24 continuous hours, and posted meter or sign limits still apply.</p></div><div class="border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Warning signs</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">The City lists signs that a vehicle may be abandoned: it never moves, leaves, needles or debris pile up on it, snow is piled on or around it, its registration expired more than 45 days ago, or it looks badly damaged or undrivable.</p></div></div><div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 border-l-4 border-l-amber-500/70 rounded-lg p-5 my-6 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Check with the neighbors first.</p>A neighbor may be sick or out of town. A quick knock on the door can solve it faster than a complaint.</div><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">How to report it in the City of Spokane</h2><ol class="space-y-4 my-6"><li class="flex gap-4"><span class="shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full border border-red-500/50 text-red-400 text-sm font-bold">1</span><div class="pt-1"><p class="font-semibold text-white">Go to <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://myspokane311.my.site.com/citizen/s/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Spokane311</a>, or call 311 or (509) 755-2489.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mt-1">Phone hours are Monday to Friday, 7 a.m. to 6 p.m. Online, choose the Parking Violation Complaint / Vehicle on the Street category.</p></div></li><li class="flex gap-4"><span class="shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full border border-red-500/50 text-red-400 text-sm font-bold">2</span><div class="pt-1"><p class="font-semibold text-white">Describe exactly where it is.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mt-1">Give the address or nearest address and which side of the street. There may be similar cars on the block, so accuracy matters.</p></div></li><li class="flex gap-4"><span class="shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full border border-red-500/50 text-red-400 text-sm font-bold">3</span><div class="pt-1"><p class="font-semibold text-white">Add the vehicle details.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mt-1">Make, model, color, and the license plate if you can see it. Leave your contact info in case the officer can't find it.</p></div></li><li class="flex gap-4"><span class="shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full border border-red-500/50 text-red-400 text-sm font-bold">4</span><div class="pt-1"><p class="font-semibold text-white">The City checks and gives notice.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mt-1">The City says it works to get the vehicle to comply. If nothing changes, the vehicle may be impounded (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/news/stories/2026/11/10/think-the-vehicle-in-front-of-your-house-is-abandoned/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane, Nov. 2026</a>). An older City article put the whole process at about a week (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://my.spokanecity.org/news/stories/2015/10/21/what-happens-to-abandoned-vehicles/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">City of Spokane</a>).</p></div></li><li class="flex gap-4"><span class="shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full border border-red-500/50 text-red-400 text-sm font-bold">5</span><div class="pt-1"><p class="font-semibold text-white">If it comes back, file a new complaint.</p><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mt-1">A car that gets moved and later returns to sit again needs a fresh report.</p></div></li></ol><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Abandoned car on your own property</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">If someone left a vehicle in your driveway, yard, or parking lot, you don't have to wait for the city. Under Washington law:</p><ul class="space-y-2 my-6"><li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="shrink-0 mt-1 text-red-400"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg></span><span class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">Residential property</strong> (a home or building with four or fewer units): a registered tow truck operator can impound an unauthorized vehicle <strong class="text-white">right away</strong> at the owner's direction (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/RCW/default.aspx?cite=46.55.010" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">RCW 46.55.010</a>).</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="shrink-0 mt-1 text-red-400"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg></span><span class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">Business and other nonresidential lots:</strong> immediately if the lot has the required towing signs at each entrance, or after <strong class="text-white">24 hours</strong> if it isn't posted (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/RCW/default.aspx?cite=46.55.010" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">RCW 46.55.010</a>, <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/RCW/default.aspx?cite=46.55.070" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">RCW 46.55.070</a>). Apartment complexes with more than four units count as nonresidential.</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="shrink-0 mt-1 text-red-400"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg></span><span class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">You sign the authorization:</strong> the property owner or their agent must sign an impound authorization at the time and place of the tow. The tow company can't sign it for you (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://app.leg.wa.gov/rcw/default.aspx?cite=46.55.080" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">RCW 46.55.080</a>).</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="shrink-0 mt-1 text-red-400"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg></span><span class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">You can't keep or sell it:</strong> the state DOL says you can't sell a vehicle abandoned on your property; a registered tow company has to remove it (<a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="https://dol.wa.gov/vehicles-and-boats/vehicles/vehicle-registration/vehicle-title/abandoned-vehicles" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">WA Department of Licensing</a>).</span></li></ul><div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 border-l-4 border-l-red-500/60 rounded-lg p-5 my-6 text-gray-300 leading-relaxed"><p class="font-semibold text-white mb-1">Make sure it really isn't authorized.</p>The law warns that whoever authorizes an improper private impound can be liable for the vehicle owner's costs (RCW 46.55.080). If you think the car may be stolen, call Crime Check at (509) 456-2233 first.</div><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Moving your own car that won't start</h2><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">Got a notice on your own car, or a car that died and has been sitting? Moving it before the deadline avoids an impound and the daily storage fees that come with one. We can connect you with a local partner tow company to take it to your home, a mechanic, or a storage spot. Online estimates use a <strong class="text-white">$140 hook fee plus $6 per mile, with a $200 minimum</strong>. See <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/towing-cost-spokane">Spokane towing cost</a> for examples, and if your car has already been towed, see <a class="text-red-500 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/towed-car-spokane">how to find a towed car in Spokane</a>.</p><h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-white mt-14 mb-5 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><div class="divide-y divide-slate-800 border-y border-slate-800"><div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">How long can a car be parked on the street in Spokane?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">Inside the City of Spokane, a passenger vehicle can't stay parked on the same block face for more than 72 continuous hours outside the downtown Paid Parking Zone, unless a sign gives a shorter limit. RVs, boats and trailers are limited to 24 hours, and so are vehicles inside the Paid Parking Zone (SMC 16A.05.310).</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">How do I report an abandoned car in Spokane?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">For a vehicle on a City of Spokane street, file a Parking Violation Complaint (the Vehicle on the Street category) online at Spokane311, or call 311 or (509) 755-2489, Monday to Friday, 7 a.m. to 6 p.m. Give the exact location, which side of the street it's on, and the make, model, color and plate if you can see them.</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">How do I report an abandoned car in Spokane Valley?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">For a vehicle on a Spokane Valley street or other city property, report it to Spokane Valley Code Enforcement through SVexpress (online or the SVexpress311 app) or call the City at (509) 720-5000. For a vehicle left on private property, Spokane Valley says to report it to Crime Check at (509) 456-2233.</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">Can I have a car towed off my own property in Washington?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">Yes. On residential property (four or fewer living units), Washington law lets a registered tow truck operator impound an unauthorized vehicle immediately at the property owner's direction (RCW 46.55.010). You have to sign an impound authorization at the time and place of the tow, and the tow company can't sign it for you (RCW 46.55.080).</p></div><div class="py-5"><h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-2">Can I keep or sell a car someone abandoned on my property?</h3><p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">No. The Washington Department of Licensing says you can't sell a vehicle abandoned on your property; you have to have a registered tow truck company remove it.</p></div></div><div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-red-600 to-red-700 rounded-2xl p-8 text-center mt-12"><h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-white">Need a Car Moved?</h3><p class="mb-6 text-white/90">We connect you with a local partner tow company. Price confirmed before dispatch.</p><a href="tel:+15097977999" class="bg-white text-red-600 px-8 py-4 rounded-xl hover:bg-gray-100 transition font-bold text-lg inline-block shadow-lg">Call (509) 797-7999</a></div>
</div>
@endsection
